  "title": "Conjugate Acids and Bases",
  "content": {
    "bibrefs": [],
    "model": [
      {
        "children": [
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "The concept of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "a conjugate acid–base pair"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "An acid and a base that differ only by the presence or absence of a proton (H"
                      },
                      {
                        "sup": true,
                        "text": "+"
                      },
                      {
                        "text": " ion)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "1980254801",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2606371200",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " is useful in describing Brønsted-Lowry acid–base reactions (and other reversible reactions, as well). When an acid donates a proton, we call the product that remains the "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "conjugate base"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "Substance formed when an acid loses a proton."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "3172405849",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "2855947944",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": " of the acid. This species is a base because it can accept a proton (to re-form the acid):"
              }
            ],
            "id": "d4gjc87sguni8o7",
            "type": "p"
          },
